AUDIO: '$329. So the claim on the table is that a 5-year-old budget card runs a frontier-shaped coding agent at speed. Every one of those four numbers can be checked against the file, not argued'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about on a forum, checked with arithmetic. One of them holds up almost exactly. One is off by a whole card class. One has no post behind it that a search can find. And the fourth, the 170,000, is true and misleading at the same time,'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which is the worst of the four because the context window a machine can hold and the context window a model can actually use are two different numbers. The screenshots going round this week measure the first one. So let us do the'

[01:04] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Diagram showing a loop of "PROPOSE", "SCAFFOLD", and "ROLLOUT" with text "The loop writes its own homework".
AUDIO: 'arithmetic properly. Ornith 1.5 went up on Hugging Face on the 18th of August and the team announced it the next day. Three models, a 9B dense, a 35B mixture of experts, and a 397B.'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'All MIT licensed, so the weights are yours and there is no bill per token. The training pitch is the unusual part. According to the team\'s own write-up, the model was not fed a fixed pile of human-written tasks. It proposes'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'progressively harder tasks itself, generates a scaffold for each one, instructions, tools, decomposition, and then produces a solution rollout. Reward from that rollout flows back through all three stages. Better policy, harder'

[01:43] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Text "Underneath all of it, a fine-tune" with "Qwen 3.5 x GEMMA 4" and "Qwen3_5MoeForConditionalGeneration" in a config.json.
AUDIO: 'tasks, better scaffolds, better training signal, round and round. It is also the kind of paragraph that makes a forum immediately suspicious. One commenter said the write-up deserved a Nobel Prize in vague, but wildly grandiose promises,'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which is unkind and not entirely wrong. Underneath all of it, this is a fine-tune. The config file names the architecture as Qwen 3.5 mixture of experts, and the community read is that'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '1.5 is post-trained on Qwen 3.6. Ornith puts that base model in its own comparison table, which answers the usual complaint about fine-tuners hiding what they started from. The 35B is where this story lives, 36 billion parameters'

[02:21] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Table showing coding benchmark scores for Ornith-1.5-35B-A3B and other models on Terminal-Bench 2.1 and SWE-Bench.
AUDIO: 'in total. 256 experts, eight of them fire on any given token, which works out to roughly 3 billion parameters doing actual work. That ratio is the entire trick. You pay for 36'

[02:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'billion in storage, and you pay for 3 billion in speed. Hold on to that, because in about 4 minutes it becomes the whole answer. The scores the vendor publishes are strong. 67.8 on Terminal Bench 2.1 under one harness, 68.5 under'

[02:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'another, 79 on SWE-Bench verified, 59.6 on S WeBench Pro. Against the base model it started from, those are wide margins, not rounding. And credit where it is'

[03:00] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Screenshot of the Terminal-Bench public leaderboard with various models and their scores.
AUDIO: 'due, because this is rarer than it should be. The footnotes under that table name the harness for each row, the temperature, the context window, and the fact that every result is the mean of five runs. The SWE-Bench rows even state'

[03:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that Git history was stripped and network access disabled, so the model could not look up its own answers. It is still the vendor\'s own measurement. Terminal Bench keeps a public leaderboard of agent and model cares,'

[03:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and as of the 20th of August, Ornith does not appear on it. Not 1.5, not 1.0, not one row, which brings us to the part you can settle for yourself, file sizes. The official GGML repository lists the'

[03:37] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Bar chart showing 21.71 GB of weights on an RTX 4090 24 GB VRAM, leaving ~2.3 GB spare.
AUDIO: 'Q4KM build of the 35B at 21.71 GB. Full precision is 71. And for a sense of what you have escaped, the 397B at the same quantization is 240 GB an'

[03:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hour. TX4090 has 24 GB of video memory, so the weights land with about 2.3 to spare, which is comfortable until you remember you have not loaded any contacts yet.'

[04:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The 3060 has 12. The weights do not fit. Roughly 10 GB of this model has to live in ordinary system memory and get read back across the bus while it works. Now put 170,000 tokens of context on top of'

[04:15] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Mathematical breakdown of KV cache cost per token: "2 x 256 x 2 x 2B FP16".
AUDIO: 'that and the whole arrangement should collapse. It does not and the reason is sitting in the config file in plain text, 40 layers and the layer list says only every fourth one is full attention.'

[04:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '10 layers keep a cache that grows as you talk. The other 30 are linear attention and their state does not grow with the conversation at all. Two key value heads, head dimension 256 keys and values, 2 bytes each at half precision.'

[04:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Multiply it out across the 10 layers that care and one token of context costs about 20 KB. So 170,000 tokens is about 3 and 1/2 GB. Quantize that cache to 8 bits, which'

[04:53] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Bar chart showing total memory usage (weights + KV cache) of 23.45 GB on an RTX 4090 24 GB.
AUDIO: 'most local setups do and it is under two. Had all 40 layers kept a cache, the same window would have cost close to 14 GB, more than a 3060 has in total, four times the memory for the same'

[05:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'conversation. That one architectural choice is the difference between a video and a joke, so the memory claim survives. 21.7 of weights plus under two of cache is 23 and 1/2 and a 4090 holds 24. It fits.'

[05:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Barely and only if you quantize the cache. Leave it at half precision and you are over the card by more than a gigabyte. Speed is harder because speed needs somebody who owns the card and is'

